conventions:
TypeScript run natively by Node 24 (type stripping) and type-checked with `tsc --strict` against the target. Only ERASABLE syntax: no enum, no namespace, no constructor parameter properties (use `as const` objects and explicit fields). The code under test is `solution.ts` with named, explicitly typed exports, in starter/, solution/ and every bugs/<name>/. Imports between files use the `.ts` extension. Tests are `tests/*.test.ts` using `node:test` + `node:assert/strict`, loading the target with:

    const { fn } = await import(`../${process.env.TARGET ?? "starter"}/solution.ts`);

Name tests descriptively: the name is what `bugs[].expect` matches. A starter stub must still type-check (e.g. `throw new Error("TODO")` in a function with the right signature). Favor the type system: discriminated unions, narrowing, generics with constraints, readonly, satisfies. A bug variant may also be a TYPE bug that only `tsc` catches; then `expect` is the tsc error text (e.g. `error TS2322`).
